[Music]  beginning in the 1950s women serving in  the u.s military could face  immediate discharge not for any  misconduct  but because they've become pregnant or  had recently given birth  that policy changed in the 1970s but  it's only in recent years that pregnant  women and young mothers  have been welcomed and accommodated in  america's armed forces  natalie brand has the story air force  major lauren daly landed a dream job on  an aircraft that flies top  government officials our top five  is our primary customer and why we exist  so that's the  first lady the vice president the  chairman of the joint chiefs the  secretary of defense and the secretary  of state  but at home she has a different title  mom  juggling life with newborn hope and  toddler jacob  when i first got into the military 10  years ago there were not a lot of  females  in the higher ranks and the ones that  were did not have children or did not  choose to have a family  to make it to where they were and now  you're kind of starting to see  like more women in the wing commander  positions and squadron commander  who have done it that's helping to shape  new policies like  one enacted in 2019 requiring nursing  mothers have access to private and  sanitary lactation  areas policy sets cultures so that's a  significant win for us  i think change takes time it's not going  to happen  overnight but i'm very optimistic it's  progress that was not in place when  lieutenant colonel jessica ruttenberg  was pregnant with her first child  nine years ago my conversations went  away from  would you like to take this more  competitive job that is more demanding  to accelerate your career  to would you like to separate at the end  of your service commitment or take this  less demanding job to become more with  your children which would have been fine  they didn't ask my husband the same  question and that's when i started  realizing  that motherhood sometimes had a little  bit of a barrier  in the institution the mother of three  who is married to a fellow air force  pilot says the military  is taking steps to combat pregnancy bias  including this memo from former defense  secretary mark  esper last year which said the  department will  update its military equal opportunity  policy to prohibit  pregnancy-based discrimination i am  setting  12 weeks of fully paid maternity leave  as the standard across the joint force  another key change increased maternity  leave  and a one-year deferment from deployment  and physical fitness testing in the air  force  a new bill proposes to expand that to  all military branches and  increase leave to 12 weeks for both  parents  the war department was designed for sort  of a 1950s model  right for a male and a stay-at-home wife  and that has changed now so we're still  working through those changes but every  day it is getting better i don't want to  worry major aliyah nadim  chairs the air force women's initiative  team are you allowed to reveal your  secret motto  yes our secret motto is get stuff done  and i am super proud of that because we  don't just talk about it we actually do  it  the dress and appearance instruction  like getting hair style standards  eased for years service women said  having to wear their hair in these tight  buns  caused headaches and hair loss now  ponytails or  braids are allowed or the development of  a maternity flight suit just last year  these might seem like small changes but  major nadim says  all together they will make the military  stronger  we know women at the 10-year mark get  out at higher rates than their male  peers  why is that and we did some research and  one of them is  family planning lack of access to child  care  it's been a slow climb to the top  leadership levels  retired general ann dunwoody became the  military's first  four star female military officer in  2008  the list of four star females in  military history  is only seven long so far each of these  women have led careers demonstrating  incomparable skill integrity and  duty to country and at every step  they've also helped push open the doors  of opportunity  to women in our military this past march  president joe biden named two female  generals to prestigious four-star  commands  if confirmed they would be just the  second and third women to lead combatant  commands  why do you think it matters to have not  only women in the military  but moms in the military well i think  that it's a retention so if you want to  keep women in the military for  a full career cycle 20 years you're  going to have to accept that they're  going to want to have children  at some point most of them are the  second reason is i think it's essential  for our national security to  have a more inclusive military i think  it makes us more lethal  service members like lieutenant colonel  ruttenberg and major daly are rising the  ranks  and realizing motherhood has not  grounded their careers but rather has  made them better leaders  so now people my age and younger are  kind of seeing like maybe i can do both  i don't have to make a choice  and get out when my commitment's over  when i want to start having a family  it's possible yes it is for cbs  this morning saturday natalie brand  washington  breaking down those barriers to access  is the key and they seem like they're  doing it  and there we say this so much but you  really do have to see it sometimes  yeah to think that that's what you want  to do know that you can be a mom  and have a career in the military and  those small changes are huge  yeah yeah thanks to natalie for that  yeah

